9. The persons engaged in the same occupation or line of business: as, the book-''trade''.

{{smaller block|All this authorship, you perceive, is anonymous; it gives me no reputation except among the ''trade''.
{{right|''Irving''. (''Imp. Dict.'')}}}}

10. A purchase or sale; a bargain; specifically, in ''U. S. politics'', a deal.

{{smaller block|But it is not every man's talent to force a ''trade;'' for a customer may choose whether he will buy or not.
{{right|''Dryden'', Duchess of York's Paper Defended.}}
Give us something like the Australian system of voting, so that the resulting legislature will represent the state's business interests, and not a series of deals, dickers, ''trades'', and bargains.
{{right|''The Century'', XXXVII. 633.}}}}

11†. The implements, collectively, of any occupation.

{{smaller block|The shepherd . . . with him all his patrimony bears,
His house and household gods, his ''trade'' of war.
{{right|''Dryden'', tr. of Virgil's Georgics, iii. 535.}}}}

12. Stuff: often used contemptuously in the sense of 'rubbish.' [Prov. Eng. and New Eng.]

{{smaller block|Ale, sir, and aqua vitæ, and such low-bred ''trade'', is all I draw now-a-days.
{{right|''Kingsley'', Westward Ho! xiv.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Balance of trade.''' See ''balance''.—'''Board of trade.''' (''a'') In the United States, an association of business men established in most large cities for the furtherance of commercial interests, the enactment of rules for the regulation of trade, and the consideration of legislation affecting banking, insurance, railroads, customs, etc.; a chamber of commerce. (''b'') [''caps.''] In Great Britain, a committee of the Privy Council which has, to a large extent, the supervision of British commerce and industry. At its head are the President of the Board of Trade, who is usually a member of the Cabinet, the parliamentary secretary (formerly vice-president), the permanent secretary, and six assistant secretaries at the head of six departments—the ''commercial, harbor, finance, railway, marine'', and ''fisheries''. Attached to the Board of Trade are}}

readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|also the bankruptcy and emigration departments, the Patent Office, etc. A committee for trade and the plantations existed for a short time in the reign of Charles II. The council of trade was again constituted in the reign of William III., but discontinued in 1782. In 1786 the Board of Trade was organized, and its functions were subsequently greatly extended.—'''Coasting-trade.''' See ''coasting.''—'''Course of trade.''' See ''course''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Fair trade,''' a proposed system of trade between Great Britain or British possessions and other countries, as advocated by the British fair-traders and the Fair-Trade League since about 1886. The fair-traders disclaim the intention of returning to protection, and aim at establishing reciprocity, and at the imposition of retaliatory duties on imports from countries which tax British products.—'''Free trade.''' See ''free.''—'''Jack of all trades.''' See ''Jack''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Round trade,''' on the Gaboon river, a kind of barter in which the things exchanged comprise a large assortment of miscellaneous articles. Also called ''bundle-trade.''—'''To blow trade,''' to blow (in) one course; blow constantly in the same direction. See ''trade-wind.''}}

{{smaller block|The wind ''blowing trade'', without an inch of sayle we spooned before the sea.
{{right|''Hakluyt's Voyages'', III. 849.}}}}

{{smaller block|'''Tricks of the trade.''' See ''trick''<sup>1</sup>.='''Syn. 6''' and '''7.''' ''Pursuit, Vocation'', etc. See ''occupation.''}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Pertaining to or characteristic of trade, or of a particular trade: as, a ''trade'' practice; a ''trade'' ball or dinner; ''trade'' organizations.—{{smaller|'''Trade dollar.''' See ''dollar.''—'''Trade price,''' the price charged by the manufacturer or publisher to dealers in the same trade for articles that are to be sold again at an advance.—'''Trade sale,''' an auction sale by manufacturers, publishers, or others of goods to the trade.}}

¶
'''trade'''<sup>1</sup> (trād), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''traded'', ppr. ''trading.'' [< ''trade''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1†.''' To take or keep one's course; pass; move; proceed.

{{smaller block|His grizly Beard a sing'd confession made
What fiery breath through his black lips did ''trade.''
{{right|''J. Beaumont'', Psyche, i. 17.}}}}

'''2.''' To engage in trade; engage in the exchange,

readings.c1s3:
purchase, or sale of goods, wares, and merchandise, or anything else; barter; buy and sell; traffic; carry on commerce as a business: with ''in'' before the thing bought and sold.

{{smaller block|This element of air which I profess to ''trade in.''
{{right|''I. Walton'', Complete Angler, p. 25.}}}}

'''3.''' To buy and sell or to exchange property in a specific instance: as, A ''traded'' with B for a horse or a number of sheep.—'''4.''' To engage in affairs generally; have dealings or transactions.

{{smaller block|How did you dare
To ''trade'' and traffic with Macbeth
In riddles and affairs of death?
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, iii. 5. 4.}}}}

'''5.''' To carry merchandise; voyage or ply as a merchant or merchantman.

{{smaller block|They shall be my East and West Indies, and I will ''trade'' to them both.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., i. 3. 79.}}}}

{{smaller|'''To trade on''', to take advantage of or make profit out of: as, to ''trade on'' another's fears.—'''Touch and trade papers.''' See ''paper''.}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1†.''' To pass; spend.

{{smaller block|Of this thyng we all beare witnesse, whom here ye see standinge, whiche haue ''traded'' our liues familiarly with him.
{{right|''J. Udall'', On Acts ii.}}}}

'''2†.''' To frequent for purposes of trade.

{{smaller block|The English merchants ''trading'' those countreys.
{{right|''Hakluyt's Voyages'', I. 458.}}}}

'''3.''' To sell or exchange in commerce; barter; buy and sell.

